PLACES=[
N('knox-school-5-thompsons-lake','Knox District School No. 5','Knox (Thompson\'s Lake)','albco','civic','standing',42.66043,-74.04486,'site','public','walk','','1898–1939',
  'A one-room district school of 1898, a 20 by 24 foot wood-frame building on a dry-laid stone foundation. It taught children until 1939 by the account of a former pupil (Wikipedia says school use ended in 1940), then stood empty. New York State Parks bought it in 1999 with the land of Thompson\'s Lake State Park, and the Kiwanis Club of the Helderbergs restored it under the Parks historic restoration office, finishing in 2014: the building was jacked up and its foundation, sills and floor joists rebuilt. It was listed on the National Register in 2005.',
  'A small clapboarded schoolhouse on Ketcham Road at the entrance to the Emma Treadwell Thacher Nature Center, joined to it by a Schoolhouse Trail. Inside, by the hilltowns history site, are desks taken apart and put back together with pupils\' carvings still on them and a donated pot-bellied stove like the original; the sources do not say the desks are the school\'s own. No source gives open days for the interior: interior not checked.',
  'A one-room school saved by a service club with a sawmill owner among its members.',
  'Owned by New York State Parks (Thompson\'s Lake Campground, part of Thacher State Park). The park is open for day use all year; the 2026 vehicle entrance fee is $7 per car (park page). Park at the nature center lot on Nature Center Way, about 550 m south, and walk the Schoolhouse Trail or the road shoulder. Park phone (518) 872-1674.',
  links=[W('Knox District School No. 5'),L('Albany Hilltowns: Knox School District No. 5','https://www.albanyhilltowns.com/Knox_School_District_No._5'),L('Albany Hilltowns: Knox School 5 restoration','https://albanyhilltowns.com/Knox_School_5_Restoration'),L('Altamont Enterprise (2014): Kiwanis complete restoration','https://altamontenterprise.com/node/4511'),L('NYS Parks: Thompson\'s Lake Campground','https://parks.ny.gov/visit/state-parks/thompsons-lake-campground-thacher-state-park')],
  conf='mixed',th=[42.65587,-74.04326],thn='Nature center lot, Nature Center Way',brick='no'),
N('district-school-7-coeymans-hollow','District School No. 7 (the Little Red Schoolhouse)','Coeymans Hollow','albco','civic','standing',42.47386,-73.90087,'site','view','drive','','1879–1957',
  'A one-room brick school built in 1879 for Coeymans school district 7, three bays by three in the Italianate style. It taught until 1957 and now houses the Little Red Schoolhouse Historical Society. It was listed on the National Register in 1996.',
  'Red brick (Wikimedia photograph, May 2010) with white-painted arches over the windows and door, a clock in the gable and an open belfry with its bell on the roof. Look also for the wide overhanging eaves and the ornate hood moulds over the door and windows. Whether and when the society opens the building was not found in any source read: interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned and used by the Little Red Schoolhouse Historical Society (Wikipedia). See it from the road; opening times not checked. The Ravena Coeymans Historical Society (a different body) keeps a museum in Ravena, open Monday and Thursday 1 to 3:30 pm and Saturday noon to 2 pm except holiday weekends, by its own page.',
  links=[W('District School No. 7'),L('Ravena Coeymans Historical Society: the town\'s Register list','https://coeymanshistory.org/historic-register/')],
  brick='yes'),
N('israel-shear-house','Israel Shear House (Shear Farm)','Coeymans Hollow','albco','landmark','standing',42.47222,-73.89889,'area','view','drive','','1780 or about 1810',
  'A Federal-style farmhouse built for Israel Shear, a timber-frame house on a rubble foundation. The dates disagree: the roadside marker calls it the 1780 Shear Farm, built by Israel Shear, while the Register summary on Wikipedia dates the house to about 1810. The marker says it was restored in 1980; it was listed on the state and National Registers in 1996.',
  'The center door is the thing to see: a broad nine-panel door between side lights, framed by Corinthian columns. A private home: from the road only.',
  '',
  'Private home on NY 143 (the marker stands on the road). View from the road; do not enter the property.',
  links=[W('Israel Shear House'),HM('1780 Shear Farm',131672)],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('acton-civill-academy-coeymans','Acton Civill Polytechnic Academy (Coeymans School)','Coeymans','albco','civic','standing',42.47294,-73.79810,'site','view','drive','','1873–1963',
  'Acton Civill (1804–1889) built this academy as a gift to Coeymans Landing, to a design by John Cornelius of Albany, at an estimated cost of $100,000. The Register form for the historic district says 1873 and Wikipedia says 1874. Civill would not let it open in his lifetime and left too little to run it: in 1889 the corporation was dissolved with $41.05 in assets, the building still unfinished inside. It was sold at auction in 1898 and bought the same year by the Coeymans school district, opened as a school in 1899 and taught until 1963. After standing empty for years it was turned into 28 apartments for older residents.',
  'A three-story Second Empire school of Croton brick under a slate mansard roof, with a four-story central tower and an unusual amount of cast iron: tower, window heads, eaves, cornices, corner quoins, porches, balustrade and chimney. The original tall two-over-two windows survive. Across Civill Street (No. 22) stands the academy\'s wood-frame dormitory of about 1873, now a house, and a war memorial is on the grounds. Apartments inside: not open.',
  'A $100,000 school that sat finished outside and empty inside for 25 years.',
  'Senior apartments at Westerlo Street and Civill Avenue: see it from the street. Do not go onto the grounds or into the building.',
  links=[W('Coeymans School'),CLHD,L('U.W. Marx: conversion to senior apartments','https://www.uwmarx.com/?p=1276')],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('reformed-dutch-church-coeymans','Former Reformed Dutch Church (1840), Church Street','Coeymans','albco','worship','standing',42.4746,-73.79469,'exact','view','drive','','1840',
  'The Reformed Protestant Dutch Church of Coeymans was formed in March 1793. This brick Greek Revival church followed in 1840; a plaque on the front reads "Reformed Dutch Church, Erected D. 1840, S.D. Lamalla Arch." The congregation later built a new church in the village of Ravena, and the old one was made into apartments in the 1970s. Up the street at No. 36 the former Methodist Episcopal church of 1836 is brick in the same style.',
  'A gable-fronted brick church with a projecting entry bay, broad brick pilasters, a fully pedimented cornice and a two-stage bell tower rising from the roof. The Register form notes Victorian window sash with hammered and colour-banded glass and several figurative and geometric stained-glass panels still in place; the double entry doors have been replaced. Apartments inside: not open.',
  '',
  'Private apartments at 24 Church Street, Coeymans Landing. See it from the street.',
  links=[CLHD],
  brick='?'),
N('mccarty-stone-cottage-coeymans','General David McCarty Stone Cottage','Coeymans','albco','landmark','standing',42.47461,-73.79213,'exact','view','walk','','about 1765–1770',
  'A one-and-a-half-story cottage of uncoursed rubble stone on a small lot on Second Street, one of the earliest streets laid out at Coeymans Landing. Its own Register form dates it to about 1770 and the historic district form to about 1765. David McCarty took part in the events before the Revolution, served in the Continental Army and held public posts afterwards; the cottage is the only thing of his known to survive, and the smallest of the three 18th-century stone houses at the Landing. Listed on the National Register in 2015.',
  'Stone walls on three sides with a stone gable on the south end, which the form calls unusual for the area, a brick fireplace back and chimney outside that gable, and a long stone high in the gable carved with letters. A strip of old bluestone sidewalk runs in front. The frame house joined to its north side may have been McCarty\'s storehouse. Private home: from the street.',
  '',
  'Private home at 29 Second Street, Coeymans Landing. View from the street only.',
  links=[L('National Register form: Brigadier General David McCarty Stone Cottage','https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/15000474_text'),CLHD],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('slingerlands-freight-station','Slingerlands D&H station (freight house) on the rail trail','Slingerlands','albco','rail','standing',42.62924,-73.86357,'site','view','walk','','1864',
  'The Albany and Susquehanna Railroad opened from Albany to Central Bridge in September 1863 and was leased to the Delaware and Hudson in 1870. At Slingerlands a combined passenger and freight station was built in 1864; in 1888 it was moved to its present spot and made the freight house when a larger passenger station went up where the firehouse now stands. Passenger trains on this stretch stopped in the 1930s and freight in the 1990s. Albany County bought the 9-mile rail bed in 2010 and built the Helderberg-Hudson Rail Trail on it, finished from Albany to Voorheesville in 2018.',
  'The old station building on Kenwood Avenue beside the trail, with an interpretive panel. The panel says the four buildings on the south side of the line on an 1893 map still stand on Kenwood Avenue. What the building is used for now was not checked: interior not checked.',
  'The 1864 depot that was moved and made the freight house.',
  'The trail is owned and run by Albany County. Park in the lot on Kenwood Avenue about 150 m west of the station and walk the trail; trail hours not checked. The building\'s owner was not checked: see it from the trail and the street.',
  links=[HM('The Slingerlands Train Station',142349),HM('Helderberg-Hudson Rail Trail',142351),W('Albany County Helderberg-Hudson Rail Trail')],
  th=[42.62881,-73.86527],thn='Lot on Kenwood Avenue',brick='?'),
N('jerusalem-reformed-church-feura-bush','Jerusalem Reformed Church','Feura Bush','albco','worship','standing',42.577006,-73.880985,'site','view','drive','','1825–1826',
  'The Jerusalem Dutch Reformed Church was established in 1790 or 1791 between Unionville and the hamlet then called Jerusalem, the first church immediately south of Albany, and gave Feura Bush its old name. Wikipedia says the church moved to Jerusalem in 1825; the marker says the present building went up in 1826. The Rev. Harmanus Van Huysen was its pastor from 1793 to 1825.',
  'A village church on NY 32 with its marker in front. The building\'s fabric and interior were not described in any source read: interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Active church on NY 32 in Feura Bush. See it from the road; service times not checked.',
  links=[HM('Jerusalem Reformed Church',130527),W('Feura Bush, New York')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('new-scotland-presbyterian-church','Presbyterian Church in New Scotland and the New Scotland Cemetery','New Scotland','albco','worship','standing',42.63139,-73.90611,'site','view','drive','','1849',
  'The congregation was founded in 1787 by the Suffolk Presbytery, the second Presbyterian church in Albany County, and incorporated in 1789; its first church was built in 1791. The present church, its second, was built in 1849 and lengthened in 1868; an education wing followed in 1957. The cemetery beside it holds about 500 burials from the 18th to the 20th century. Listed on the National Register in 2010.',
  'A two-story frame church with a projecting entrance block and a large square tower of two stages, and the old burying ground. One marker here says "Original frame standing" of the 1791 church without saying where. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Active church on New Scotland Road (NY 85). See it and the cemetery from the road; access to the cemetery and service times not checked.',
  links=[W('Presbyterian Church in New Scotland and the New Scotland Cemetery'),HM('New Scotland Church',142757),HM('Early Mission',142748)],
  brick='no'),
N('preston-hollow-baptist-church','Baptist Church of Preston Hollow','Preston Hollow, Rensselaerville','albco','worship','standing',42.44308,-74.20586,'area','view','drive','','1846',
  'The congregation began as the "First Church of Rensselaerville", in existence by 1787 and organized about 1793; the marker credits it with proposing, in 1801, the first American Baptist missionary society and a national Baptist assembly. A church built on Fox Creek in 1821 was taken down in 1833, a second near the schoolhouse served until 1846, and the present church was built that year. The church took the Preston Hollow name in 1842.',
  'The church and its marker on NY 145 in the hamlet named for Dr. Samuel Preston, who settled here in 1789 and built the first sawmill and gristmill (another marker). Building materials and interior not described in any source read: interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Listed as a current church by the hilltowns history site. See it from NY 145; service times not checked.',
  links=[HM('1787 Baptist Church',241630),L('Albany Hilltowns: Baptist Church of Preston Hollow','https://albanyhilltowns.com/Baptist_Church_of_Preston_Hollow'),HM('Dr. Samuel Preston',131419)],
  brick='?'),
N('westerlo-hamlet','Westerlo hamlet (old Chesterville)','Westerlo','albco','town','standing',42.51518,-74.04168,'area','view','drive','','about 1800',
  'The hamlet on NY 143 was once called Chesterville. The town of Westerlo was formed on March 16, 1815 from Coeymans and Rensselaerville and named for the Rev. Eilardus Westerlo of Albany; its first town meeting was held in William Beardsley\'s house on April 4, 1815. The Reformed church, the first church in the town, was organized in 1793 at a site about five miles south near Dormansville and later moved here; the Baptist church was organized at Chesterville in 1800.',
  'A string of state and local markers along NY 143 and Myers Road: the Reformed Church, the Baptist Church, the house that was the Chesterville Academy (built about 1800, Gabriel Pinney headmaster), the site of a three-story hotel (burned 1897, rebuilt, razed about 1953), and Philip Myers\'s farm, where he built a log house in 1763. The building dates of the two churches were not found: interior not checked.',
  '',
  'A public road through a hamlet; the churches and houses are private or in church use. Park where it is legal along NY 143 and read the markers from the shoulder.',
  links=[HM('Westerlo',135147),HM('Reformed Church',135148),HM('Academy',135154),HM('Baptist Church',135162),HM('Hotel',135156),HM('Myers Farm',135149),HM('Reformed Church (original site, Dormansville)',136003)],
  brick='?'),
N('teunis-houghtaling-house','Teunis Houghtaling House (Vredehuis)','Clarksville','albco','landmark','standing',42.56474,-73.97500,'site','view','drive','','about 1770',
  'A hand-hewn timber-frame farmhouse built in two stages: the main block about 1770 (the marker says before 1770), widened to five bays about 1790 when a one-story addition was also built. Teunis Houghtaling was born in 1740 and died in 1806. Two frame outbuildings and a small family cemetery with burials from the early 19th century are on the property. Listed on the National Register in 2004.',
  'An 18th-century farmhouse with its marker on County Route 312. Private: see it from the road. The family cemetery is on private land.',
  '',
  'Private home on County Route 312 south of Clarksville. View from the road only.',
  links=[W('Teunis Houghtaling House'),HM('Houghtaling House',135121)],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('clarksville-hamlet','Clarksville hamlet on the Delaware Turnpike','Clarksville, New Scotland','albco','town','standing',42.57585,-73.9628,'area','view','drive','','about 1810',
  'A hamlet on the Delaware Turnpike (NY 443) named on April 16, 1833 for Adam A. Clark, innkeeper and first Clarksville postmaster (1826–1841). Harmanus Bogardus built houses here: the Meed House of about 1810 held the first Bethlehem post office under Henry L. Meed (1812–1826), and Bogardus\'s own house of about 1803 burned in the Great Fire of 1918.',
  'On NY 443: the Clark House, built about 1828 for Adam and Betsy Clark, and the Meed House of about 1810 at Tarrytown Road, each with a marker; a two-sided marker on the north side gives the site of the Bogardus house and of a blacksmith shop built about 1832 over Cave Creek. Private houses: from the road.',
  '',
  'Public road through the hamlet; the houses are private. See them from NY 443.',
  links=[HM('Clark House',142470),HM('Meed House',142469),HM('Harmanus Bogardus House / Blacksmith Shop',142472)],
  brick='?'),
N('altamont-maple-avenue','Altamont\'s old business street (Knowersville) after the 1886 fire','Altamont','albco','town','standing',42.7025,-74.0334,'area','view','walk','','1871',
  'Altamont grew as Knowersville, named for Benjamin Knower, an Albany merchant who bought land and built a hat factory about 1800. On April 30, 1886 fire leveled all five buildings of the Lockwood estate on the business street, among them a carriage factory, a shoe store, a tin shop, a drug store and two lodge rooms; the fight for fire protection that followed led to the village\'s incorporation and a fire company (1893). Most of the row was rebuilt within months.',
  'A set of bilingual village markers on Maple Avenue and Lincoln Avenue: St. John\'s Lutheran Church (1871–72, Albany architect Charles Nichols), the first church in Knowersville; the carriage works, rebuilt in 1886, a garage from 1917 and apartments since 1942; the Enterprise building, home of the weekly newspaper since 1901; the firehouse and village hall of 1932; and the 1879 Lincoln Avenue School, moved a block west in 1904. The 1897 D&H station (the library) is a short walk away. Interiors not checked.',
  '',
  'Village streets; the buildings are private, church or village property. Park on the street where it is legal and walk.',
  links=[HM('The Great Fire',131465),HM('The Carriage Works',131468),HM('Enterprise Building',131455),HM('St. John\'s Lutheran Church',131427),HM('Fire Department/Village Hall',131453),HM('Lincoln Avenue School',131471),HM('Knowersville',133581)],
  brick='?'),
]
